WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.911 --> 00:00:15.391
In Search of Dharma.

2
00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:19.271
6: When Dharmas Go Wrong.

3
00:00:21.491 --> 00:00:25.191
Every failure of a dharma turns out to be
the dark twin of a strength.

4
00:00:25.911 --> 00:00:30.031
The bond that holds a group together is
the fence that shuts others out;

5
00:00:30.151 --> 00:00:34.091
the authority that can free people is the
authority that can rule them;

6
00:00:34.431 --> 00:00:36.831
the practice that liberates is the cage.

7
00:00:37.671 --> 00:00:39.731
Failure is not a flaw in a dharma;

8
00:00:39.811 --> 00:00:43.831
it is the strength, over-extended -- or
worn as a mask.

9
00:00:45.971 --> 00:00:48.551
In the Buddhist-majority country of
Myanmar,

10
00:00:48.591 --> 00:00:51.851
a monk named Ashin Wirathu spent years
preaching.

11
00:00:52.246 --> 00:00:53.891
He wore the saffron robe.

12
00:00:54.131 --> 00:00:55.711
He had taken the vows.

13
00:00:56.371 --> 00:00:59.471
And what he preached, to large and adoring
crowds,

14
00:00:59.471 --> 00:01:02.191
was hatred of his Muslim neighbours, the
Rohingya,

15
00:01:02.311 --> 00:01:05.031
in terms so incendiary that he came to be
seen,

16
00:01:05.031 --> 00:01:08.971
not entirely unfairly, as the Burmese face
of a coming slaughter.

17
00:01:09.531 --> 00:01:10.911
When that slaughter came,

18
00:01:10.911 --> 00:01:14.611
when soldiers and mobs drove more than a
million Rohingya from their homes,

19
00:01:14.671 --> 00:01:16.891
burning villages and killing as they went,

20
00:01:16.931 --> 00:01:19.971
the man in the robe had helped lay the
moral groundwork,

21
00:01:19.971 --> 00:01:21.991
in the name of protecting the faith.

22
00:01:23.864 --> 00:01:25.391
Buddhism, in the Western imagination,

23
00:01:25.931 --> 00:01:28.871
is the gentle dharma, the one with no
crusades,

24
00:01:28.871 --> 00:01:33.191
the religion of loving-kindness, the
breath, and the patient smile.

25
00:01:33.691 --> 00:01:36.911
And here is a Buddhist monk, sincere,
learned, devout,

26
00:01:37.431 --> 00:01:40.711
using that very tradition to bless an
ethnic cleansing.

27
00:01:41.351 --> 00:01:46.291
The first instinct, the comforting one, is
to say: well, he is not a real Buddhist.

28
00:01:47.071 --> 00:01:48.611
Real Buddhism would never.

29
00:01:49.031 --> 00:01:53.591
But that instinct is a trap, and refusing
it is where we have to begin.

30
00:01:55.455 --> 00:01:58.611
We have spent five parts in something
close to admiration.

31
00:01:59.551 --> 00:02:01.951
We have watched how dharmas hold us
together,

32
00:02:01.951 --> 00:02:04.351
how they answer the deepest human needs,

33
00:02:04.411 --> 00:02:08.071
how they get so far inside us that they
feel like truth itself.

34
00:02:08.771 --> 00:02:11.291
We have mostly been sympathetic witnesses.

35
00:02:12.051 --> 00:02:14.391
We cannot stay sympathetic any longer,

36
00:02:14.391 --> 00:02:18.171
because a sympathetic account that never
looked at the wreckage would be a lie,

37
00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:20.771
and would deserve none of the trust it
asks for.

38
00:02:21.391 --> 00:02:24.871
If dharmas do everything the last five
parts said they do,

39
00:02:24.891 --> 00:02:27.931
why is so much of history's cruelty signed
in their name?

40
00:02:28.747 --> 00:02:33.691
So now we look straight at that harm, done
sincerely, century after century,

41
00:02:33.691 --> 00:02:37.691
by people every bit as certain of their
own goodness as we are of ours.

42
00:02:40.831 --> 00:02:43.691
Why "that's not real Buddhism" is a
cop-out

43
00:02:46.171 --> 00:02:48.731
The comforting exit has to be closed
first:

44
00:02:49.031 --> 00:02:52.331
almost everyone reaches for it, and it
leads nowhere.

45
00:02:54.431 --> 00:02:58.891
When a Buddhist monk incites genocide, or
a Christian blesses a slave ship,

46
00:02:58.951 --> 00:03:01.131
or any tradition is turned to cruelty,

47
00:03:01.131 --> 00:03:05.191
the reflex is to declare that the
perpetrators have betrayed the true faith,

48
00:03:05.191 --> 00:03:10.071
that real Buddhism or real Christianity or
real Islam is the peaceful thing

49
00:03:10.071 --> 00:03:12.731
and the violence is a corruption from
outside.

50
00:03:13.051 --> 00:03:15.151
Philosophers have a name for this move.

51
00:03:15.351 --> 00:03:18.091
They call it the No True Scotsman fallacy,

52
00:03:18.371 --> 00:03:20.611
after the imaginary patriot who insists

53
00:03:20.631 --> 00:03:23.711
that no true Scotsman would do some
disgraceful thing,

54
00:03:23.751 --> 00:03:26.851
and who, when shown a Scotsman who did
exactly that,

55
00:03:27.011 --> 00:03:29.691
simply replies that no true Scotsman
would.

56
00:03:29.991 --> 00:03:33.151
The trick is that you protect your ideal
by redefining anyone

57
00:03:33.151 --> 00:03:35.471
who violates it as not really a member.

58
00:03:37.271 --> 00:03:41.271
Scholars at Harvard's Religious Literacy
Project put the correction bluntly:

59
00:03:41.431 --> 00:03:43.411
religions are internally diverse,

60
00:03:43.471 --> 00:03:45.611
and a tradition deep enough and old enough

61
00:03:45.611 --> 00:03:48.631
to contain a saint is deep enough to
contain a butcher,

62
00:03:48.671 --> 00:03:52.611
with both sincerely drawing on the same
scriptures and the same authority.

63
00:03:53.151 --> 00:03:54.491
The peace-preaching monk

64
00:03:54.491 --> 00:03:57.491
and the hate-preaching monk are both
authentically Buddhist.

65
00:03:57.991 --> 00:04:01.531
To say so is not to say the tradition
commends them equally;

66
00:04:01.571 --> 00:04:05.811
it is to refuse the referee's chair, which
no outside observer holds.

67
00:04:06.291 --> 00:04:10.191
To wave the second away as fake is loyalty
dressed up as argument,

68
00:04:10.191 --> 00:04:11.971
a way of refusing to see.

69
00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:17.591
So I am going to hold to an uncomfortable
rule from here on.

70
00:04:18.399 --> 00:04:21.691
The failures of a dharma are not betrayals
of its pure essence.

71
00:04:22.151 --> 00:04:24.611
They are expressions of its real
potential,

72
00:04:24.671 --> 00:04:28.631
as genuine as its glories, and they grow
from the very same roots.

73
00:04:32.267 --> 00:04:33.271
The dark twin

74
00:04:36.043 --> 00:04:40.111
The last five parts turned up a handful of
things that make a dharma powerful.

75
00:04:40.974 --> 00:04:44.491
A strong, identity-shaping sense of who
"we" are.

76
00:04:45.251 --> 00:04:49.591
Costly, hard-to-fake commitment that binds
people tightly together.

77
00:04:50.551 --> 00:04:54.791
A transcendent authority that outranks any
mere person or law.

78
00:04:56.031 --> 00:04:57.391
Deep in-group trust.

79
00:04:58.311 --> 00:05:02.671
And an installation machinery, the ritual
and habit and repetition,

80
00:05:02.671 --> 00:05:07.651
that drives all of this below conscious
thought until it feels like simple reality.

81
00:05:08.671 --> 00:05:12.911
Every one of those is a genuine strength,
and exactly why dharmas work.

82
00:05:14.631 --> 00:05:17.711
Part 5 ended on the blankness of the last
of these:

83
00:05:18.337 --> 00:05:21.051
the installation machinery will groove
cruelty

84
00:05:21.051 --> 00:05:25.011
into the bone exactly as faithfully as it
grooves kindness.

85
00:05:25.471 --> 00:05:27.691
The same blankness runs the whole list.

86
00:05:28.655 --> 00:05:33.631
A strong "we" is a wonderful thing right
up until you ask what happens to "them."

87
00:05:34.451 --> 00:05:39.331
Costly commitment binds the just community
and the death cult with equal strength.

88
00:05:39.651 --> 00:05:43.311
A transcendent authority can chastise a
tyrant or crown him.

89
00:05:43.751 --> 00:05:46.831
Deep trust inside the group is the same
psychological fact

90
00:05:46.831 --> 00:05:48.931
as deep suspicion of those outside it.

91
00:05:49.311 --> 00:05:52.331
The features and the failures are not two
separate lists.

92
00:05:52.971 --> 00:05:55.651
They are one list, seen from two sides.

93
00:05:56.851 --> 00:06:00.551
Every failure of a dharma is the dark twin
of one of its strengths,

94
00:06:00.731 --> 00:06:02.831
which is why no dharma is safe,

95
00:06:02.911 --> 00:06:05.171
and why the failures keep coming back no

96
00:06:05.171 --> 00:06:07.551
matter how often we are scandalised by
them.

97
00:06:08.071 --> 00:06:10.031
Six of these twins are worth naming.

98
00:06:12.964 --> 00:06:14.491
The fence becomes a wall

99
00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:19.291
The first and deepest failure is the one
we

100
00:06:19.291 --> 00:06:21.531
have been circling since the very
beginning,

101
00:06:21.531 --> 00:06:23.811
the hard edge around the warm circle.

102
00:06:25.551 --> 00:06:29.291
A dharma works by turning a crowd of
strangers into an "us."

103
00:06:29.471 --> 00:06:30.831
That is its genius,

104
00:06:30.831 --> 00:06:32.831
the thing that lets it hold a group
together

105
00:06:32.831 --> 00:06:35.691
across a scale no troop of apes could
manage.

106
00:06:36.191 --> 00:06:40.911
But an "us" is defined by its boundary,
and a boundary has an outside.

107
00:06:41.451 --> 00:06:45.211
The same bonding that makes you cherish
your own people is the very mechanism

108
00:06:45.211 --> 00:06:48.591
that primes you to fear and distrust the
people beyond the line.

109
00:06:49.211 --> 00:06:52.991
(Field studies add one mercy: the two
faces can come apart,

110
00:06:52.991 --> 00:06:56.611
and the hostility often tracks deprivation
more than devotion.)

111
00:06:58.111 --> 00:07:00.711
Researchers call it parochial altruism:

112
00:07:00.771 --> 00:07:04.871
we make sacrifices for the in-group and
brace ourselves against the out-group.

113
00:07:05.571 --> 00:07:09.031
Turned up, fed on fear, and pointed at a
hated minority,

114
00:07:09.071 --> 00:07:12.231
that ordinary instinct becomes the engine
of the pogrom,

115
00:07:12.231 --> 00:07:15.371
the crusade, the inquisition, and the
cleansing.

116
00:07:15.751 --> 00:07:19.091
The fence that holds the community
together becomes the wall behind

117
00:07:19.091 --> 00:07:21.151
which the community does its killing.

118
00:07:21.751 --> 00:07:24.831
Wirathu's monstrousness was not foreign to
his dharma.

119
00:07:25.331 --> 00:07:27.451
It was his dharma's in-group warmth,

120
00:07:27.451 --> 00:07:31.171
with the sign flipped outward at the
people who had been defined as not-us.

121
00:07:31.791 --> 00:07:33.631
The scholar Mark Juergensmeyer,

122
00:07:33.631 --> 00:07:36.831
surveying religious violence across every
major tradition,

123
00:07:36.891 --> 00:07:39.231
found the same grim pattern everywhere:

124
00:07:39.411 --> 00:07:42.431
there is no faith so peaceful that it
cannot be turned,

125
00:07:42.431 --> 00:07:45.211
by someone sincere, into a reason to kill.

126
00:07:46.905 --> 00:07:49.031
I do not want to leave this at despair,

127
00:07:49.071 --> 00:07:51.671
because one hopeful thing is buried in the
research.

128
00:07:52.411 --> 00:07:56.191
It is not obvious that the lethal version
of the boundary is hardwired.

129
00:07:56.871 --> 00:08:01.011
The primatologist Richard Wrangham points
out that our chimpanzee cousins,

130
00:08:01.051 --> 00:08:03.531
who kill each other at rates much like
ours,

131
00:08:03.631 --> 00:08:08.311
show none of the self-sacrificing
battlefield heroism that human war runs on,

132
00:08:08.611 --> 00:08:09.871
and that the readiness to die

133
00:08:09.871 --> 00:08:13.731
for the group against another group looks
more like something cultures install

134
00:08:14.031 --> 00:08:15.631
than something genes demand.

135
00:08:16.271 --> 00:08:20.731
If the killing is installed rather than
bred in the bone, then it is not fate:

136
00:08:21.011 --> 00:08:23.851
argument cannot undo it, as Part 5 warned,

137
00:08:23.911 --> 00:08:27.031
but the same slow machinery that installed
it can move it.

138
00:08:27.471 --> 00:08:29.751
The fence, in other words, can be moved.

139
00:08:30.331 --> 00:08:33.331
The line around "us" is not fixed at the
tribe;

140
00:08:33.331 --> 00:08:36.831
it has been pushed outward again and again
across history,

141
00:08:36.911 --> 00:08:39.471
from the band to the city to the nation,

142
00:08:39.671 --> 00:08:43.571
and there is no law of nature that says it
cannot be pushed further.

143
00:08:43.791 --> 00:08:47.371
It is the thin thread of hope that Part 8
will try to pull.

144
00:08:51.050 --> 00:08:54.271
The authority that frees becomes the
authority that rules

145
00:08:57.018 --> 00:09:01.351
The second twin lives in the very thing
that lets a dharma stand above mere power:

146
00:09:01.584 --> 00:09:03.671
its claim to a higher authority.

147
00:09:05.651 --> 00:09:08.371
Morality seems to have started as a
levelling device.

148
00:09:09.431 --> 00:09:11.951
In the small forager bands of the deep
past,

149
00:09:11.991 --> 00:09:14.911
the moral sense, as Christopher Boehm
argued,

150
00:09:14.931 --> 00:09:17.931
worked as a way for the many to gang up on
the would-be bully

151
00:09:17.951 --> 00:09:20.891
and keep any one person from lording it
over the rest.

152
00:09:21.591 --> 00:09:24.631
And then, when societies grew and
stratified,

153
00:09:24.631 --> 00:09:26.871
and some people came to rule over others,

154
00:09:26.871 --> 00:09:30.591
that same moral authority was captured and
turned inside out,

155
00:09:30.651 --> 00:09:34.451
made into a justification for the very
domination it had once resisted.

156
00:09:35.211 --> 00:09:37.751
This is the double face of dharma and
power,

157
00:09:37.751 --> 00:09:40.771
and the history of religion cannot be
understood without it.

158
00:09:42.671 --> 00:09:45.651
On one face, a dharma sanctifies the
ruler.

159
00:09:46.211 --> 00:09:49.891
The Hindu doctrine of caste, wedded to the
idea of karma,

160
00:09:49.931 --> 00:09:53.811
could tell the man born into the lowest
station that he had earned it cosmically,

161
00:09:54.151 --> 00:09:58.531
by his conduct in a previous life, and
that his suffering was therefore just.

162
00:09:59.171 --> 00:10:01.951
Kings across the world have ruled by
divine right.

163
00:10:02.891 --> 00:10:05.071
Marx had a hard phrase for this function:

164
00:10:05.171 --> 00:10:08.331
religion as "the opium of the people," the
drug

165
00:10:08.331 --> 00:10:11.831
that makes an unjust order feel bearable
and even holy.

166
00:10:12.431 --> 00:10:17.151
The Italian theorist Antonio Gramsci saw
the subtler version:

167
00:10:17.351 --> 00:10:19.251
the deepest power is not the soldier

168
00:10:19.251 --> 00:10:22.591
who forces you but the worldview, often
religious,

169
00:10:22.771 --> 00:10:27.051
that makes the existing arrangement feel
natural, inevitable, and right,

170
00:10:27.091 --> 00:10:31.491
so that you consent to your own place in
it without anyone having to raise a hand.

171
00:10:32.031 --> 00:10:35.451
And whoever controls the dharma controls
something even more basic,

172
00:10:35.571 --> 00:10:39.251
the power to say what counts as orthodox
and what as heresy,

173
00:10:39.331 --> 00:10:41.971
who is inside and who is to be cast out.

174
00:10:42.491 --> 00:10:47.111
In the sociologist Pierre Bourdieu's phrase,
it is the power of legitimate naming,

175
00:10:47.151 --> 00:10:50.211
and it is one of the most jealously
guarded prizes there is.

176
00:10:52.134 --> 00:10:56.991
But there is another face, and it is the
reason this is a real fight, not a swindle.

177
00:10:57.491 --> 00:11:01.551
The very same higher authority that can
crown a king can also condemn him.

178
00:11:02.291 --> 00:11:04.931
Once it is granted that there is a justice
above the throne,

179
00:11:05.291 --> 00:11:08.071
every prophet and reformer has a place to
stand.

180
00:11:08.631 --> 00:11:11.951
The Hebrew prophets used it to denounce
their own corrupt rulers.

181
00:11:12.651 --> 00:11:14.971
We watched this face at work in Part 3:

182
00:11:14.971 --> 00:11:18.031
the same machinery that let the Samin of
Java quietly

183
00:11:18.031 --> 00:11:20.771
refuse an extractive authority is the
machinery

184
00:11:20.771 --> 00:11:22.911
that caste used to sanctify one.

185
00:11:23.891 --> 00:11:26.031
The Indian reformer Bhimrao Ambedkar,

186
00:11:26.331 --> 00:11:30.371
leading half a million Dalits out of
Hinduism and into a reconstructed Buddhism,

187
00:11:30.991 --> 00:11:34.071
performed the most surgical version of
this move imaginable.

188
00:11:34.979 --> 00:11:38.271
To turn the dharma against the caste
oppression he had suffered,

189
00:11:38.271 --> 00:11:40.811
he deliberately cut out the doctrine of
karma,

190
00:11:40.811 --> 00:11:44.231
the exact teaching that had been used to
justify that oppression.

191
00:11:45.251 --> 00:11:47.451
The authority that the powerful seize

192
00:11:47.451 --> 00:11:52.751
to sanctify themselves is the same authority
the powerless seize to indict them.

193
00:11:53.871 --> 00:11:57.171
It is just that the powerful usually own
the institutions,

194
00:11:57.311 --> 00:12:01.511
so the sanctifying face is the default and
the liberating face is the harder,

195
00:12:01.511 --> 00:12:03.371
rarer, more dangerous achievement.

196
00:12:05.491 --> 00:12:08.511
And even the liberating face is not as
clean as it looks,

197
00:12:08.731 --> 00:12:12.751
which is the unsettling lesson the
philosopher Michel Foucault pressed hardest.

198
00:12:13.319 --> 00:12:16.931
Power, he argued, is not only the boot
that holds you down;

199
00:12:16.951 --> 00:12:21.211
it is also productive, busy making the
very kind of person you become.

200
00:12:21.631 --> 00:12:26.431
Run a dharma's apparatus through that lens
and the point lands close to home.

201
00:12:26.651 --> 00:12:29.431
The confessional, the spiritual director,

202
00:12:29.471 --> 00:12:32.551
the meditation hall, the programme of
self-improvement:

203
00:12:32.731 --> 00:12:35.691
these actively shape selves, train
desires,

204
00:12:35.691 --> 00:12:38.411
and produce a particular sort of
disciplined soul.

205
00:12:38.791 --> 00:12:41.371
So when a dharma frees you from one
regime,

206
00:12:41.371 --> 00:12:43.391
it tends to install another in its place,

207
00:12:43.791 --> 00:12:46.911
a fresh set of rules about how to watch
and govern yourself.

208
00:12:47.491 --> 00:12:50.071
There is no escape into a dharma-free
clearing

209
00:12:50.171 --> 00:12:52.711
where the self runs wild and unsupervised,

210
00:12:52.731 --> 00:12:56.011
because the self is itself something a
dharma builds.

211
00:12:56.511 --> 00:12:59.391
The real question is never power against
freedom.

212
00:12:59.671 --> 00:13:03.231
It is always a matter of which regime of
self-making you are living in,

213
00:13:03.331 --> 00:13:06.071
and whether you chose it or were simply
born into it.

214
00:13:09.211 --> 00:13:11.151
The cage: who holds the key

215
00:13:13.851 --> 00:13:18.271
The third twin is the way a dharma, which
promises a path to the good life,

216
00:13:18.271 --> 00:13:19.511
can become a prison,

217
00:13:19.531 --> 00:13:23.251
and nowhere is this clearer than in the
two arenas of gender and money.

218
00:13:25.091 --> 00:13:29.811
Consider gender first, because dharmas
encode it early and the record is bleak.

219
00:13:30.551 --> 00:13:32.351
Across tradition after tradition,

220
00:13:32.351 --> 00:13:35.351
the codified mainstream has placed women
under men

221
00:13:35.351 --> 00:13:37.291
and called it the order of things.

222
00:13:37.831 --> 00:13:42.031
The Hindu lawbook of Manu states flatly
that a woman must never be independent,

223
00:13:42.031 --> 00:13:45.291
passing from the control of her father to
her husband to her sons.

224
00:13:46.111 --> 00:13:50.011
An early Buddhist rule, one the canon
itself may have acquired late,

225
00:13:50.011 --> 00:13:54.411
made every nun, however senior,
subordinate to every monk, however junior.

226
00:13:54.831 --> 00:13:59.171
The Confucian "three obediences" bound a
woman to obey her father,

227
00:13:59.171 --> 00:14:01.191
then her husband, then her son.

228
00:14:01.751 --> 00:14:06.051
The household codes of the Abrahamic
scriptures lock women into submission.

229
00:14:06.651 --> 00:14:10.591
In each case the subordination is handed
the highest possible warrant,

230
00:14:10.631 --> 00:14:11.971
cosmic or scriptural,

231
00:14:11.971 --> 00:14:16.171
so that a human arrangement of power gets
to wear the costume of eternal truth.

232
00:14:17.811 --> 00:14:22.211
And yet the very same traditions carry
within themselves the materials of revolt.

233
00:14:22.995 --> 00:14:25.891
The oldest collection of women's poetry in
India is a set

234
00:14:25.891 --> 00:14:29.331
of verses by Buddhist nuns celebrating
their own awakening.

235
00:14:30.051 --> 00:14:34.611
The Hindu women-saints of the devotional
tradition, Mirabai and Akka Mahadevi,

236
00:14:34.611 --> 00:14:37.391
walked out of their marriages and their
assigned roles,

237
00:14:37.451 --> 00:14:41.651
using love of God as the one pretext their
society could not deny them.

238
00:14:42.406 --> 00:14:46.111
The same Christian scripture that tells
wives to submit also declares

239
00:14:46.111 --> 00:14:49.111
that in Christ there is "neither male nor
female."

240
00:14:49.131 --> 00:14:52.731
In our own time the scholar Amina Wadud
rereads the Qur'an

241
00:14:52.731 --> 00:14:56.891
from a woman's standpoint and argues that
its true principle is equality,

242
00:14:56.891 --> 00:15:00.071
and that the patriarchy is the accretion,
not the core.

243
00:15:00.631 --> 00:15:05.111
The rule is this: a dharma in itself
neither frees women nor cages them.

244
00:15:05.451 --> 00:15:08.311
It is the contested ground on which that
fight is waged,

245
00:15:08.831 --> 00:15:12.471
and the same scripture hands down both the
chains and the keys.

246
00:15:13.571 --> 00:15:16.331
What decides which one you get is not the
text.

247
00:15:17.571 --> 00:15:19.611
It is who holds the power to read it.

248
00:15:21.801 --> 00:15:24.751
The word "cage" carries my own values
inside it.

249
00:15:25.871 --> 00:15:27.711
The yardstick I have been using,

250
00:15:27.711 --> 00:15:31.291
that subordinating women is a failure and
freeing them a success,

251
00:15:31.311 --> 00:15:34.651
is itself a fairly modern and broadly
liberal standard,

252
00:15:34.911 --> 00:15:38.211
and I should not pretend it floats above
all dharmas as neutral fact.

253
00:15:39.171 --> 00:15:42.751
Many women, including many who had every
chance to choose otherwise,

254
00:15:42.751 --> 00:15:45.291
have described lives inside traditional
roles not

255
00:15:45.291 --> 00:15:47.431
as a prison but as a place of meaning,

256
00:15:47.431 --> 00:15:49.731
dignity, and chosen devotion.

257
00:15:50.071 --> 00:15:52.851
A fair reckoning has to hold two things at
once.

258
00:15:53.251 --> 00:15:56.271
It must register that self-understanding
honestly,

259
00:15:56.471 --> 00:15:59.211
rather than sneering at it from a
comfortable distance,

260
00:15:59.491 --> 00:16:01.471
and it must still name the documented,

261
00:16:01.471 --> 00:16:04.651
systematic ways these systems have
stripped women of autonomy

262
00:16:04.651 --> 00:16:07.731
and authority and called the result the
will of heaven.

263
00:16:08.031 --> 00:16:09.311
Both of those are true,

264
00:16:09.311 --> 00:16:11.791
and the strain between them is exactly the
kind of

265
00:16:11.791 --> 00:16:14.071
thing the question of judgement will have
to face.

266
00:16:15.831 --> 00:16:17.871
Money tells the same double story.

267
00:16:18.311 --> 00:16:22.651
Dharmas have both blessed and damned
economic orders, often in the same breath.

268
00:16:23.051 --> 00:16:27.571
The caste system assigned each person
their economic place by sacred duty.

269
00:16:27.691 --> 00:16:31.431
In the West, as the philosopher Michael
Sandel has argued,

270
00:16:31.431 --> 00:16:32.891
the old Protestant idea

271
00:16:32.891 --> 00:16:37.331
that worldly success was a sign of God's
favour, once God fell away,

272
00:16:37.471 --> 00:16:40.751
slowly curdled into the modern faith in
meritocracy:

273
00:16:40.811 --> 00:16:43.831
the conviction that the winner deserves to
win and,

274
00:16:43.831 --> 00:16:46.711
more cruelly, that the loser deserves to
lose.

275
00:16:47.151 --> 00:16:51.051
That is a secular dharma doing the oldest
dirty job in the book,

276
00:16:51.111 --> 00:16:54.471
re-sanctifying inequality after the gods
have gone home,

277
00:16:54.571 --> 00:16:57.911
telling the poor their poverty is a
verdict on their worth.

278
00:16:59.471 --> 00:17:03.411
But dharmas have also been the sharpest
critics of economic cruelty.

279
00:17:03.871 --> 00:17:07.191
David Graeber spent his career as an
anthropologist arguing

280
00:17:07.191 --> 00:17:11.471
that the language of debt and the language
of sin grew up entangled,

281
00:17:11.491 --> 00:17:13.351
and that debt has been the supreme tool

282
00:17:13.351 --> 00:17:17.131
for making the exploited feel that their
suffering is their own fault.

283
00:17:17.611 --> 00:17:20.791
Against that, he set the older and almost
forgotten intuition

284
00:17:20.831 --> 00:17:24.851
that the truly sacred act was never
repaying a debt but cancelling it,

285
00:17:24.851 --> 00:17:27.091
the clean slate, the jubilee.

286
00:17:27.311 --> 00:17:30.971
The dharma that tells the poor to accept
their lot and the dharma

287
00:17:30.971 --> 00:17:34.631
that tells them to tear up the ledger are
written in the same hand.

288
00:17:38.121 --> 00:17:39.611
The fire goes out

289
00:17:42.681 --> 00:17:44.881
The fourth twin turns the danger inward.

290
00:17:45.401 --> 00:17:48.311
Not every failure of a dharma involves a
victim.

291
00:17:49.021 --> 00:17:51.821
Some of the most insidious are the ones
where the dharma rots

292
00:17:51.821 --> 00:17:55.101
from the inside while everyone keeps going
through the motions,

293
00:17:55.341 --> 00:17:57.561
and these failures wear a friendly face,

294
00:17:57.761 --> 00:18:00.511
which is exactly what makes them so hard
to see.

295
00:18:01.221 --> 00:18:04.251
They come in two forms: the fire can go
out,

296
00:18:04.371 --> 00:18:06.671
leaving the form perfectly preserved and
empty,

297
00:18:06.901 --> 00:18:09.171
or it can burn too hot in the wrong
direction,

298
00:18:09.481 --> 00:18:11.961
feeding on the group's own certainty of
its goodness.

299
00:18:13.801 --> 00:18:15.341
The first is the hollowing.

300
00:18:15.751 --> 00:18:20.281
The sociologist Max Weber described how
the white-hot insight of a founder,

301
00:18:20.281 --> 00:18:23.501
the living fire of a Buddha or a Christ or
a prophet,

302
00:18:23.681 --> 00:18:28.181
gets slowly cooled and boxed up over the
generations into something manageable:

303
00:18:28.181 --> 00:18:30.341
the teaching codified into texts,

304
00:18:30.441 --> 00:18:32.721
the experience replaced by a ritual,

305
00:18:32.721 --> 00:18:35.821
the personal charisma succeeded by a
bureaucracy.

306
00:18:36.201 --> 00:18:38.921
The form survives, perfectly preserved,

307
00:18:38.961 --> 00:18:42.201
long after the thing it was built to carry
has died inside it.

308
00:18:42.621 --> 00:18:46.081
A worshipper can kneel in a tradition that
has become pure habit,

309
00:18:46.161 --> 00:18:49.001
recite words that mean nothing to anyone
in the room,

310
00:18:49.021 --> 00:18:51.161
and mistake the husk for the kernel.

311
00:18:51.581 --> 00:18:56.941
At the scale of a single person this rot
has a name too: spiritual bypassing,

312
00:18:56.961 --> 00:19:01.941
the use of lofty spiritual ideas to dodge
one's actual messy human problems,

313
00:19:02.041 --> 00:19:05.301
the serene meditator who is really just
fleeing his own anger

314
00:19:05.301 --> 00:19:07.421
and calling the flight enlightenment.

315
00:19:07.901 --> 00:19:12.081
In each case the practice continues while
its purpose silently inverts.

316
00:19:14.001 --> 00:19:18.841
The second self-corruption is stranger,
because the dharma's own success causes it.

317
00:19:19.481 --> 00:19:22.401
Doing good, it turns out, can license
doing bad.

318
00:19:23.261 --> 00:19:26.601
Psychologists have documented what they
call moral licensing:

319
00:19:27.041 --> 00:19:29.361
having established our virtue in one act,

320
00:19:29.421 --> 00:19:32.381
we feel quietly entitled to cut a corner
in the next,

321
00:19:32.481 --> 00:19:35.821
as though we had banked moral credit and
were now free to spend it.

322
00:19:36.581 --> 00:19:40.701
A dharma, by this logic, generates moral
capital by the ton,

323
00:19:40.701 --> 00:19:45.521
and that capital becomes a permit (the
laboratory finding is about individuals;

324
00:19:45.761 --> 00:19:49.061
scaling it up to whole communities is my
own extrapolation).

325
00:19:49.601 --> 00:19:53.321
The community certain of its own
righteousness is the community best able

326
00:19:53.321 --> 00:19:54.961
to excuse its own cruelty,

327
00:19:54.981 --> 00:19:59.141
because surely we, the good people, could
not really be the ones doing wrong.

328
00:19:59.781 --> 00:20:01.201
And the very commitment-signalling

329
00:20:01.201 --> 00:20:04.281
that binds a group can run away into a
purity spiral,

330
00:20:04.281 --> 00:20:07.901
an escalating contest to be the most
devout and the most orthodox,

331
00:20:08.121 --> 00:20:12.621
until the dharma turns inward and begins
devouring its own insufficiently faithful.

332
00:20:13.061 --> 00:20:15.941
Here the failure is not that anyone
abandoned the dharma.

333
00:20:16.401 --> 00:20:19.401
It is that they took it too seriously in
the wrong direction,

334
00:20:19.401 --> 00:20:21.781
and let their goodness become their alibi.

335
00:20:24.711 --> 00:20:26.661
The blueprint becomes the bulldozer

336
00:20:29.161 --> 00:20:31.361
Everything in this book turns on a
capacity

337
00:20:31.361 --> 00:20:34.281
that belongs on any honest list of a
dharma's strengths:

338
00:20:34.881 --> 00:20:37.621
a dharma can be held up and known as a
path.

339
00:20:38.721 --> 00:20:42.961
It can be named, questioned, reformed, and
even built on purpose.

340
00:20:43.421 --> 00:20:46.561
We watched Ambedkar do exactly that a few
pages ago,

341
00:20:46.561 --> 00:20:50.081
editing a tradition with surgical
deliberateness to free his people,

342
00:20:50.101 --> 00:20:53.661
and Part 8 will argue that we must learn
to do such building well.

343
00:20:54.031 --> 00:20:57.621
So it matters enormously that this
strength has a dark twin of its own,

344
00:20:57.841 --> 00:21:01.621
and that this twin has the highest body
count of any failure in this part.

345
00:21:03.341 --> 00:21:07.021
The French revolutionaries tried it first,
and most literally.

346
00:21:07.701 --> 00:21:11.321
In the spring of 1794 the National
Convention,

347
00:21:11.321 --> 00:21:14.841
at Robespierre's urging, decreed a new
religion into existence:

348
00:21:15.101 --> 00:21:16.901
the Cult of the Supreme Being,

349
00:21:16.941 --> 00:21:20.261
a rationalist faith of civic festivals and
public virtue,

350
00:21:20.321 --> 00:21:23.161
designed to replace the Church it had just
torn down.

351
00:21:23.681 --> 00:21:27.261
The inaugural festival drew hundreds of
thousands to the Champ de Mars,

352
00:21:27.721 --> 00:21:30.181
with a statue of Atheism burned in effigy

353
00:21:30.181 --> 00:21:33.261
and an artificial holy mountain built for
the occasion.

354
00:21:33.701 --> 00:21:38.721
The revolutionaries had already rebuilt
time itself: history restarted at Year One,

355
00:21:38.721 --> 00:21:39.941
the months renamed,

356
00:21:40.041 --> 00:21:44.341
the seven-day week and its Sunday replaced
by a rational week of ten days.

357
00:21:44.861 --> 00:21:46.641
It was a dharma by decree,

358
00:21:46.721 --> 00:21:49.881
complete with a freshly engineered
installation machinery.

359
00:21:50.641 --> 00:21:54.241
Seven weeks after the festival,
Robespierre went to the guillotine,

360
00:21:54.301 --> 00:21:56.721
and his religion went into the ground with
him,

361
00:21:56.761 --> 00:22:00.061
vanishing so completely that most people
now have never heard of it.

362
00:22:00.661 --> 00:22:03.761
The lesson is the one that Part 5 should
lead us to expect.

363
00:22:04.281 --> 00:22:05.401
What takes generations

364
00:22:05.461 --> 00:22:09.261
to sink beneath consciousness cannot be
legislated in a season,

365
00:22:09.261 --> 00:22:13.241
and a dharma that exists only as decree
dies with the man who decreed it.

366
00:22:14.861 --> 00:22:17.621
The Bolsheviks understood that, and aimed
deeper:

367
00:22:17.801 --> 00:22:21.221
not new rituals for old people, but a new
kind of person.

368
00:22:21.921 --> 00:22:24.781
Their leading theoretician, Nikolai
Bukharin,

369
00:22:24.781 --> 00:22:27.461
said it with a frankness that still stops
the breath,

370
00:22:27.561 --> 00:22:30.901
praising "proletarian compulsion in all
its forms,

371
00:22:30.901 --> 00:22:32.641
beginning with summary execution,"

372
00:22:32.981 --> 00:22:37.021
as "a method of reworking the human
material of the capitalistic epoch

373
00:22:37.021 --> 00:22:38.661
into Communist humanity."

374
00:22:38.721 --> 00:22:43.121
(The words reach us through a hostile
witness: the anarchist Alexander Berkman,

375
00:22:43.321 --> 00:22:46.361
who had gone to Russia as a believer in
the revolution,

376
00:22:46.441 --> 00:22:49.601
quoted them in the indictment he spent the
rest of his life writing.)

377
00:22:50.241 --> 00:22:51.281
Human material.

378
00:22:51.741 --> 00:22:56.201
The New Soviet Man was to be manufactured,
and the factory was the labour camp.

379
00:22:57.041 --> 00:22:59.621
Seventy years later the project was
abandoned,

380
00:22:59.621 --> 00:23:01.841
having reworked nothing and killed
millions;

381
00:23:02.181 --> 00:23:04.821
Berkman's summary of the party's basic
premise was

382
00:23:04.821 --> 00:23:08.861
that the people were a mob who "can be
made free only by compulsion."

383
00:23:10.241 --> 00:23:11.641
And then the limit case.

384
00:23:12.181 --> 00:23:14.981
On the seventeenth of April 1975,

385
00:23:15.001 --> 00:23:18.401
the Khmer Rouge took Phnom Penh and
declared Year Zero:

386
00:23:18.421 --> 00:23:20.481
history would begin again from nothing.

387
00:23:20.961 --> 00:23:23.281
Within about three days they had emptied a
city of

388
00:23:23.281 --> 00:23:26.461
two million people at gunpoint, hospitals
included.

389
00:23:26.861 --> 00:23:30.901
Money, markets, schools, religion and the
family were abolished;

390
00:23:30.941 --> 00:23:34.621
the banks were dynamited, worthless
currency raining on the streets.

391
00:23:35.561 --> 00:23:40.601
My old lecturer Paul Stange, whose scholarship
on Java runs through this whole book,

392
00:23:40.761 --> 00:23:43.581
called it "a virtually incomprehensible
effort

393
00:23:43.581 --> 00:23:46.181
to eliminate everything that had come from
outside."

394
00:23:46.441 --> 00:23:48.681
Historians still dispute the recipe:

395
00:23:48.741 --> 00:23:52.301
imported Maoism, a warped indigenous
purification,

396
00:23:52.381 --> 00:23:56.381
a countryside already shattered by years
of US American bombing;

397
00:23:56.381 --> 00:23:58.721
the erasure itself no one disputes.

398
00:23:59.501 --> 00:24:03.701
In under four years the regime killed
nearly two million of its own people,

399
00:24:03.761 --> 00:24:05.541
roughly a quarter of the country.

400
00:24:06.221 --> 00:24:11.201
The purest blank slate ever attempted was
the fastest catastrophe in this part.

401
00:24:13.361 --> 00:24:17.041
James C Scott, the scholar of peasant
resistance from Part 3,

402
00:24:17.041 --> 00:24:21.661
spent a career studying these disasters,
and his diagnosis is precise.

403
00:24:22.481 --> 00:24:26.681
The catastrophe is not planning as such;
it requires a combination.

404
00:24:27.361 --> 00:24:29.761
A blueprint drawn in perfect confidence.

405
00:24:30.741 --> 00:24:33.061
Contempt for the inherited, practical,

406
00:24:33.061 --> 00:24:35.541
local knowledge by which people actually
live.

407
00:24:36.161 --> 00:24:40.061
A state willing to use the full weight of
its force to impose the design.

408
00:24:40.621 --> 00:24:43.161
And a society too beaten down to resist.

409
00:24:44.021 --> 00:24:47.641
Run the three cases through that lens and
a gradient appears:

410
00:24:47.641 --> 00:24:50.041
the Jacobins tried to replace the liturgy,

411
00:24:50.281 --> 00:24:52.621
the Soviets tried to replace the person,

412
00:24:52.661 --> 00:24:55.001
the Khmer Rouge tried to replace
everything,

413
00:24:55.141 --> 00:24:57.701
and the body count rises with the depth of
the erasure.

414
00:24:58.701 --> 00:25:01.601
The failure, note carefully, is not
building.

415
00:25:02.301 --> 00:25:05.801
Every dharma we have met to this point was
built by someone.

416
00:25:06.301 --> 00:25:10.581
The failure is the blank slate: the
conviction that the inherited substrate,

417
00:25:10.581 --> 00:25:14.561
all that slow evolved and accumulated
material of the first five parts,

418
00:25:14.601 --> 00:25:17.921
is rubble to be cleared rather than the
only material there is.

419
00:25:19.701 --> 00:25:21.861
I grew up in a working-class Perth

420
00:25:22.021 --> 00:25:25.221
where communists were not a spectre but
part of the landscape.

421
00:25:25.821 --> 00:25:31.281
My father was a metalworker; the shop
stewards and union officials of my childhood,

422
00:25:31.361 --> 00:25:36.021
Party members among them, were some of the
most solidly decent people I have known,

423
00:25:36.181 --> 00:25:40.421
and the Left they belonged to was an
ecumenical world in which communists,

424
00:25:40.541 --> 00:25:43.921
Labor people and even the anarchists all
knew one another

425
00:25:43.961 --> 00:25:46.201
and worked together more often than not.

426
00:25:46.801 --> 00:25:48.621
As a young man I spent some years

427
00:25:48.621 --> 00:25:51.661
as an activist in the Communist Party of
Australia myself,

428
00:25:51.861 --> 00:25:55.661
working out of the same Trades Hall, and I
am not ashamed of a day of it;

429
00:25:55.661 --> 00:25:58.501
for its time and place, I still think it
was right.

430
00:25:59.211 --> 00:26:02.561
I can say that with a straight face here,
of all places,

431
00:26:02.621 --> 00:26:04.001
because the party I belonged

432
00:26:04.001 --> 00:26:07.301
to was not a branch office of the terror
described above.

433
00:26:07.861 --> 00:26:09.301
It was the counter-case.

434
00:26:09.641 --> 00:26:11.101
By the time I found it,

435
00:26:11.121 --> 00:26:12.741
it had cut its ties to Moscow

436
00:26:12.881 --> 00:26:16.641
and had publicly condemned the Soviet
tanks when they rolled into Prague,

437
00:26:16.641 --> 00:26:20.101
and it spent its last decades slowly
unbuilding its own dogma,

438
00:26:20.201 --> 00:26:23.041
arguing itself out of one certainty after
another,

439
00:26:23.101 --> 00:26:28.541
until in 1991 it did the thing no catastrophic
dharma in this section ever managed:

440
00:26:28.821 --> 00:26:33.141
it judged that its own form had outlived
its purpose and voted to dissolve itself,

441
00:26:33.201 --> 00:26:36.821
its people and their politics flowing on,
by fits and starts,

442
00:26:36.921 --> 00:26:39.901
into the movement that inherited their
ground: the Greens.

443
00:26:42.441 --> 00:26:44.681
The two pictures belong side by side:

444
00:26:44.701 --> 00:26:47.261
the whole lesson of this twin sits between
them.

445
00:26:47.641 --> 00:26:50.641
The same word covers the emptied city of
Year Zero

446
00:26:50.641 --> 00:26:53.121
and the unionists I knew in Trades Hall,

447
00:26:53.121 --> 00:26:57.621
and what separates them was never the
dream of a fairer world, which both held.

448
00:26:58.021 --> 00:26:59.201
It was the boot.

449
00:26:59.511 --> 00:27:04.021
A constructed dharma that renounces the
boot can keep the power to unbuild,

450
00:27:04.021 --> 00:27:07.541
and every horror in this section lacked
exactly that power.

451
00:27:07.961 --> 00:27:10.941
(None of the gentler builders ever
commanded a state,

452
00:27:10.941 --> 00:27:13.321
so the comparison is not a controlled one;

453
00:27:13.321 --> 00:27:16.181
it is still the only comparison history
offers.)

454
00:27:16.521 --> 00:27:19.061
Dharmas can be built; they always were.

455
00:27:19.421 --> 00:27:24.241
But never with a boot, never at gunpoint,
never against the grain of the animal,

456
00:27:24.261 --> 00:27:26.581
and never without leaving the unbuilding
power

457
00:27:26.841 --> 00:27:29.341
in the hands of the people the dharma is
for.

458
00:27:32.311 --> 00:27:34.761
The mirror: dharma in the yoga studio

459
00:27:37.241 --> 00:27:41.381
The sixth twin is the gentlest-looking and
closest to home.

460
00:27:41.781 --> 00:27:45.541
Part 1 opened with the word "dharma" on
the front of a yoga studio,

461
00:27:45.541 --> 00:27:50.141
a soft and pleasant Western thing, vaguely
Eastern, vaguely spiritual.

462
00:27:50.521 --> 00:27:55.101
It is time to look at that studio
squarely, because it is a failure mode too,

463
00:27:55.101 --> 00:27:58.541
the failure of how a dharma travels across
a line of power.

464
00:28:00.201 --> 00:28:02.901
The problem is not that the West borrowed
from the East.

465
00:28:03.721 --> 00:28:05.301
Cultures have always borrowed,

466
00:28:05.301 --> 00:28:09.081
and dharmas have always lived and grown by
crossing borders and mixing;

467
00:28:09.241 --> 00:28:12.661
there were Greek kings on the frontier of
India two thousand years ago

468
00:28:12.661 --> 00:28:15.161
who put the wheel of the dharma on their
coins.

469
00:28:15.801 --> 00:28:17.261
Borrowing is not theft.

470
00:28:17.801 --> 00:28:19.381
The trouble is in the terms.

471
00:28:19.861 --> 00:28:23.401
When a powerful culture takes from a
culture it once colonised,

472
00:28:23.421 --> 00:28:26.621
strips away the demanding ethical and
communal core,

473
00:28:26.621 --> 00:28:30.541
keeps only the soothing technique, and
sells it back as a product,

474
00:28:30.741 --> 00:28:34.521
something has gone wrong that the innocent
word "sharing" does not cover.

475
00:28:36.161 --> 00:28:38.101
That deep structure has a name,

476
00:28:38.101 --> 00:28:42.381
given its critical sense by the scholar
Edward Said: Orientalism,

477
00:28:42.381 --> 00:28:47.001
the long habit of the West constructing
the East as an exotic, mysterious,

478
00:28:47.161 --> 00:28:48.961
faintly unreal Other,

479
00:28:48.981 --> 00:28:52.541
a screen onto which to project whatever
the West was hungry for.

480
00:28:53.001 --> 00:28:55.461
The meditation app that promises stress
relief

481
00:28:55.501 --> 00:28:58.581
with none of the inconvenient teaching
about selflessness

482
00:28:58.801 --> 00:29:00.601
and the renunciation of craving,

483
00:29:00.621 --> 00:29:03.181
the mindfulness reduced to a productivity
hack

484
00:29:03.181 --> 00:29:05.961
for the very economy it was once meant to
question,

485
00:29:05.961 --> 00:29:07.881
has been called, sharply,

486
00:29:07.881 --> 00:29:12.041
"McMindfulness," a nickname the critic
Ronald Purser made stick.

487
00:29:12.481 --> 00:29:15.181
It keeps the calm and discards the
challenge.

488
00:29:16.861 --> 00:29:19.241
The appropriation goes deeper than
products,

489
00:29:19.381 --> 00:29:21.761
all the way down into the categories
themselves.

490
00:29:22.341 --> 00:29:23.841
The tidy nouns we reach for,

491
00:29:24.141 --> 00:29:28.841
"Buddhism," "Hinduism," and the very idea
of a list of "world religions," are

492
00:29:28.841 --> 00:29:32.781
to a considerable degree
nineteenth-century European inventions.

493
00:29:33.741 --> 00:29:38.041
They carve up other people's lives in ways
those people did not always recognise,

494
00:29:38.241 --> 00:29:41.941
built on Christian assumptions about what
a religion is even supposed to be.

495
00:29:42.721 --> 00:29:45.041
The West did not only borrow the
practices.

496
00:29:45.401 --> 00:29:47.281
It manufactured the containers through

497
00:29:47.281 --> 00:29:50.241
which we now perceive "Eastern religion"
in the first place,

498
00:29:50.341 --> 00:29:52.601
and then forgot they were manufactured.

499
00:29:52.781 --> 00:29:55.981
So when we sort the world's dharmas into
their neat boxes,

500
00:29:56.061 --> 00:30:00.221
we are often handling tools originally
forged as instruments of empire.

501
00:30:01.841 --> 00:30:03.881
And now the sting I cannot avoid,

502
00:30:03.921 --> 00:30:07.061
because the non-privileging eye has to
turn back on itself.

503
00:30:07.481 --> 00:30:11.421
This very project, a secular dharma
assembled in a Western language

504
00:30:11.421 --> 00:30:12.861
from traditions elsewhere,

505
00:30:12.861 --> 00:30:15.461
is a prime suspect in its own
investigation.

506
00:30:16.021 --> 00:30:18.481
When I lift a "universal core" out of
Buddhism

507
00:30:18.481 --> 00:30:22.041
and set its Asian cosmology aside as
cultural packaging,

508
00:30:22.041 --> 00:30:24.841
I am doing the exact move an appropriator
would do,

509
00:30:24.841 --> 00:30:28.341
and saying the exact thing an appropriator
would say to justify it.

510
00:30:29.221 --> 00:30:30.841
Whether that is honest translation

511
00:30:30.861 --> 00:30:34.081
or quiet theft is not settled by my good
intentions.

512
00:30:34.501 --> 00:30:36.901
It turns on power, on reciprocity,

513
00:30:37.221 --> 00:30:40.581
on whether I keep the demanding parts or
only the pleasant ones,

514
00:30:40.641 --> 00:30:45.101
and on whether I am willing to be changed
by what I borrow or only to consume it.

515
00:30:45.341 --> 00:30:47.901
I do not get to exempt myself from this.

516
00:30:48.361 --> 00:30:49.381
No one does.

517
00:30:52.821 --> 00:30:54.041
The wolf in the flock

518
00:30:56.361 --> 00:30:59.761
Every failure we have walked through so
far shares one feature,

519
00:30:59.761 --> 00:31:03.481
and it is the feature that makes them so
disturbing: they are sincere.

520
00:31:03.701 --> 00:31:05.961
The fence-builder loves his people;

521
00:31:06.121 --> 00:31:09.261
the priest who crowns the king believes in
the crown;

522
00:31:09.341 --> 00:31:12.201
the keeper of the cage thinks he is
preserving order;

523
00:31:12.301 --> 00:31:16.721
the planner who empties a city believes,
with all his heart, in the blueprint;

524
00:31:16.741 --> 00:31:20.721
and even the appropriator, myself
included, mostly means well.

525
00:31:21.741 --> 00:31:23.861
The dark twins need no villain.

526
00:31:24.861 --> 00:31:29.181
Ordinary devotion, turned a few degrees,
does the damage by itself.

527
00:31:31.601 --> 00:31:35.101
But a dharma is not only vulnerable to its
sincere believers.

528
00:31:36.161 --> 00:31:38.021
The apparatus that makes it work,

529
00:31:38.021 --> 00:31:41.501
the deep trust it manufactures and the
benefit of the doubt it extends

530
00:31:41.501 --> 00:31:43.921
to anyone who performs the right
devotions,

531
00:31:44.121 --> 00:31:47.921
is a standing invitation to a figure who
is not sincere at all.

532
00:31:48.261 --> 00:31:50.361
Back in Part 2, costly,

533
00:31:50.401 --> 00:31:52.321
hard-to-fake signals sorted the committed

534
00:31:52.321 --> 00:31:55.161
from the casual and welded a group
together.

535
00:31:55.881 --> 00:31:58.641
That filter was built to keep out the
free-rider,

536
00:31:58.641 --> 00:32:01.881
the one who wants the warmth of belonging
without paying for it.

537
00:32:02.281 --> 00:32:04.441
It works against almost everyone.

538
00:32:04.821 --> 00:32:06.881
It does not work against the rare person

539
00:32:06.881 --> 00:32:10.421
who can perform every signal flawlessly
and feel nothing behind it.

540
00:32:12.761 --> 00:32:14.101
Such people exist.

541
00:32:15.321 --> 00:32:19.041
The clinical literature has described the
far end of the range since

542
00:32:19.041 --> 00:32:24.141
the psychiatrist Hervey Cleckley named the
"mask of sanity" in 1941:

543
00:32:24.441 --> 00:32:27.621
a stretch of the human range (as Part 5
said,

544
00:32:27.621 --> 00:32:29.581
not a separate kind of human being,

545
00:32:29.601 --> 00:32:32.251
nor a headcount any two instruments agree
on) in

546
00:32:32.581 --> 00:32:34.801
whom the ordinary equipment of conscience,

547
00:32:34.881 --> 00:32:39.821
empathy, fear, and remorse is, from early
on, muted close to silence.

548
00:32:40.561 --> 00:32:43.101
But the hunting ground does not check
diagnoses;

549
00:32:43.341 --> 00:32:47.021
what stocks it is the structure, not the
rarity of such people.

550
00:32:47.461 --> 00:32:52.301
A dharma produces trust in bulk and hands
it to whoever wears the robe convincingly,

551
00:32:52.301 --> 00:32:56.421
so the devotions can be worn as a costume,
clinical profile or none,

552
00:32:56.441 --> 00:32:59.361
by anyone able to perform them without
feeling their pull.

553
00:32:59.821 --> 00:33:02.241
This is the guru who preys on his
disciples,

554
00:33:02.241 --> 00:33:05.201
the venerated elder unmasked after thirty
years,

555
00:33:05.201 --> 00:33:08.161
the holy man whose followers cannot credit
the charges precisely

556
00:33:08.161 --> 00:33:10.101
because he played the part so well.

557
00:33:10.611 --> 00:33:12.581
He does not over-extend a strength.

558
00:33:13.181 --> 00:33:14.181
He wears it.

559
00:33:15.921 --> 00:33:18.421
Here the deep past speaks straight to the
present.

560
00:33:19.021 --> 00:33:23.181
The forager bands of Part 2 had a remedy
for exactly this figure:

561
00:33:23.181 --> 00:33:26.081
the intimidator who could not be shamed
was watched,

562
00:33:26.161 --> 00:33:28.841
levelled, and in the last resort removed,

563
00:33:29.041 --> 00:33:32.781
because in a camp of thirty there is
nowhere for a predator to hide.

564
00:33:33.441 --> 00:33:36.801
What the small band could do, the
mega-society cannot.

565
00:33:37.301 --> 00:33:40.001
The very anonymity that broke the old
morality,

566
00:33:40.001 --> 00:33:42.741
the sea of strangers a dharma was built to
cross,

567
00:33:42.861 --> 00:33:45.861
is also the condition under which the
predator flourishes,

568
00:33:45.921 --> 00:33:46.921
because the transparency

569
00:33:46.921 --> 00:33:50.901
that once exposed him cannot reach across
a congregation of thousands

570
00:33:50.921 --> 00:33:53.081
or a corporation of tens of thousands.

571
00:33:53.801 --> 00:33:57.521
What protected the band was never a gift
for detecting insincerity;

572
00:33:57.521 --> 00:33:59.241
it was accountable authority:

573
00:33:59.241 --> 00:34:03.481
everyone visible, everyone levellable, and
the settled power to remove.

574
00:34:03.981 --> 00:34:08.721
Any community that manufactures trust at
scale owes its members the modern forms of

575
00:34:08.721 --> 00:34:09.921
that same equipment.

576
00:34:10.541 --> 00:34:15.121
And it owes them one memory more: this
same equipment has served, in every age,

577
00:34:15.121 --> 00:34:16.341
to punish mere difference,

578
00:34:16.661 --> 00:34:20.681
so a community that hunts wolves must
watch what else gets called a wolf.

579
00:34:22.501 --> 00:34:25.501
We built dharmas so that strangers could
trust strangers,

580
00:34:25.701 --> 00:34:28.121
and in the same stroke we built the ideal
cover

581
00:34:28.121 --> 00:34:30.381
for the stranger who should never be
trusted.

582
00:34:30.941 --> 00:34:33.921
And this undoes the comfort sincerity
seemed to offer:

583
00:34:33.941 --> 00:34:36.921
the sincere butcher may be more troubling
than the fake one,

584
00:34:36.921 --> 00:34:40.321
but it does not follow that sincerity is a
test we can lean on.

585
00:34:41.121 --> 00:34:43.921
Sincerity is the very thing the predator
counterfeits,

586
00:34:44.021 --> 00:34:45.021
and a dharma's demand

587
00:34:45.021 --> 00:34:48.561
for visible devotion rewards the most
fluent counterfeiter in the room.

588
00:34:51.581 --> 00:34:52.941
No dharma is safe

589
00:34:55.401 --> 00:34:59.341
Taken together, these twins tell a single
and rather severe truth.

590
00:35:01.181 --> 00:35:05.341
The bond that holds a community together
is the fence that shuts the stranger out.

591
00:35:05.921 --> 00:35:09.541
The authority that lets a prophet shame a
tyrant is the authority

592
00:35:09.541 --> 00:35:11.461
that lets a priest crown one.

593
00:35:12.321 --> 00:35:17.681
A path that promises a good life can trap
half of humanity inside an assigned role.

594
00:35:18.261 --> 00:35:23.141
The very fire that gives a dharma life is
the fire that gutters out into empty form,

595
00:35:23.141 --> 00:35:26.281
or flares up into a righteousness that
licenses cruelty.

596
00:35:27.121 --> 00:35:29.321
The capacity that lets a people know,

597
00:35:29.361 --> 00:35:32.681
reform and build its own path is the
capacity that,

598
00:35:32.721 --> 00:35:34.561
armed with a state and a blueprint,

599
00:35:34.561 --> 00:35:37.941
will bulldoze a living world to make room
for a paper one.

600
00:35:38.581 --> 00:35:41.641
The exchange that lets dharmas grow is the
extraction

601
00:35:41.641 --> 00:35:44.721
that strip-mines a conquered people's
wisdom for a product.

602
00:35:46.521 --> 00:35:49.481
In every case the failure is not a foreign
contamination

603
00:35:49.761 --> 00:35:51.761
that a purer practice could avoid.

604
00:35:52.221 --> 00:35:55.281
It is the strength itself, running past
its limit.

605
00:35:55.981 --> 00:36:00.421
Even the predator who games a dharma from
outside marks the rule's edge:

606
00:36:00.541 --> 00:36:03.921
not a strength over-extended but a
strength counterfeited,

607
00:36:04.081 --> 00:36:06.641
possible only because the strengths are
real.

608
00:36:07.221 --> 00:36:08.901
That means the comforting dream,

609
00:36:08.901 --> 00:36:13.041
the dream of finding the one good dharma
that does not fail, is an illusion.

610
00:36:13.561 --> 00:36:15.081
There is no such dharma.

611
00:36:15.631 --> 00:36:19.561
The tendency to fail is the standing price
of a dharma being powerful enough

612
00:36:19.561 --> 00:36:20.981
to do anything at all.

613
00:36:22.801 --> 00:36:25.561
This is bleak, but it is also clarifying,

614
00:36:25.621 --> 00:36:28.821
because it finally kills a question we
should never have been asking.

615
00:36:29.581 --> 00:36:33.241
"Is it a dharma?" was never the same
question as "is it good?".

616
00:36:33.721 --> 00:36:36.721
A thing can be fully, authentically a
dharma,

617
00:36:36.941 --> 00:36:41.421
comprehensive and binding and deep, and be
an instrument of horror.

618
00:36:42.021 --> 00:36:44.241
So we cannot rest in the comfortable
pluralism

619
00:36:44.241 --> 00:36:47.421
that says every path is one more lovely
flower in the garden.

620
00:36:47.921 --> 00:36:49.341
Some of these flowers are poison.

621
00:36:50.361 --> 00:36:51.921
If failure is intrinsic,

622
00:36:52.021 --> 00:36:56.461
then the only question left is not "which
dharma is pure," because none is,

623
00:36:56.721 --> 00:36:59.681
but "which dharma fails least, and
recovers best,

624
00:36:59.681 --> 00:37:02.201
and by what standard could we possibly
say."

625
00:37:02.421 --> 00:37:06.161
That is the question of judgement, the one
we have been walking towards,

626
00:37:06.181 --> 00:37:08.101
and we can no longer walk around it.

627
00:37:09.981 --> 00:37:11.921
But one complication remains.

628
00:37:12.641 --> 00:37:14.841
Every dharma we have put on trial here,

629
00:37:14.841 --> 00:37:19.721
every caste order and household code and
crusading faith, was built long ago,

630
00:37:19.721 --> 00:37:23.341
for a world that no longer exists: the
world of the village,

631
00:37:23.441 --> 00:37:27.281
the empire, the kinship clan, the agrarian
field.

632
00:37:27.901 --> 00:37:29.221
That world is gone.

633
00:37:29.831 --> 00:37:34.041
For hundreds of millions of people now,
the old gods have fallen silent,

634
00:37:34.201 --> 00:37:36.961
and the village has been replaced by the
restless, rootless,

635
00:37:37.521 --> 00:37:40.481
screen-lit anonymity of the modern city
and the internet.

636
00:37:41.001 --> 00:37:43.301
So the question is about to become double.

637
00:37:43.771 --> 00:37:48.781
Not only how we are to judge dharmas, but
also what becomes of dharma itself now,

638
00:37:48.781 --> 00:37:51.321
in an age that has misplaced the
foundations on

639
00:37:51.321 --> 00:37:53.431
which every previous dharma was built.

